  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Deep upper level low pressure continues \nto circulate off the California coast this morning and moisture \nfrom the south will continue to push north. Favorable dynamics \nwill produce isolated to scattered showers with the likelihood of\na few rumbles of thunder by this afternoon, primarily within \ncentral Nevada and portions of eastern Nevada as activity moves \nin from the south as indicated. Forecast surface- based CAPE \nvalues will be in the 100 to perhaps 150J/kg range with weak \ninstability denoted by slightly negative lifted indicies. Daytime \nhigh temperatures will be in the low 60s to low 70s with overnight\nlows primarily in the 30s.\n\nWednesday thru Friday, the upper closed low will slowly and \ngradually move inland on Wednesday. From here, latest numerical\nmodel solutions indicate that the low will weaken into an open\nwave and cross into southern and central Nevada Thursday and \nFriday with the trof axis situated directly over Nevada Thursday\nnight. Model trends have been consistent in showing this to be a \nwarm storm with snow levels above 8000 feet. Rainfall amounts in \nthe valleys Wednesday thru Friday remain relatively robust at\naround a half- inch with some valley accumulations nearing an \ninch within portions of central Nevada. Daytime high temperatures during\nthis period will be warm on Wednesday with readings still in the \nlow 60s to low 70s, but cooling around ten degrees by Thursday\nwith low 50s to low 60s forecast. Temperatures will be a few more\ndegrees cooler Friday. Winds will be breezy to locally windy each\nafternoon, especially in central Nevada where gusts to 30-40 mph \nare expected. Overnight lows cool to the mid 20s to mid 30s for \nThursday night, becoming mid teens to mid 20s by Friday night.\n\nThe upper system is expected to shift east and out of the \nforecast area Saturday with dry conditions expected into early\nnext week. Temperatures warm a few degrees thru the weekend,\nresiding near seasonal normals early next week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Significant changes to PoP and\nweather fields thru the short term. Confidence in the short term \nforecast remains high, though there are slight differences in the \ntiming and location of convective activity Tuesday and Wednesday.",
